window.TAROT_TOOL={"cfg":{"guide":"Nyx","ctaLabel":"Talk to a live psychic now","ctaHref":"/go/","sitePopup":true,"art":{"back":true,"cards":{"the-fool":true,"the-magician":true,"the-high-priestess":true,"the-empress":true,"the-emperor":true,"the-hierophant":true,"the-lovers":true,"the-chariot":true,"strength":true,"the-hermit":true,"wheel-of-fortune":true,"justice":true,"the-hanged-man":true,"death":true,"temperance":true,"the-devil":true,"the-tower":true,"the-star":true,"the-moon":true,"the-sun":true,"judgement":true,"the-world":true},"w":640,"h":960},"w":640,"h":960,"ratio":1.5,"site":"Talk to Psychic Free"},"cards":[{"slug":"the-fool","name":"The Fool","number":"0","element":"Air","astrology":"Uranus","keywords_upright":["Fresh starts","Open-hearted trust","Spontaneity","Innocence","A leap of faith"],"keywords_reversed":["Recklessness","Hesitation at the edge","Naivety","Poor planning","Fear of beginning"],"summary":"The Fool invites you to step toward something new before every detail is mapped. It pairs openness and curiosity with a reminder to watch where your feet land.","upright":["The Fool is the card of zero, the moment before anything has been written. When it appears upright, it usually points to a beginning that has not yet taken shape: a move, a course, a relationship, a creative project, a decision to stop waiting. The figure does not know how the path will unfold, and the card does not ask you to know either. What it asks for is willingness. There is a freshness here that experienced people often envy and sometimes forget they ever had.","Traditionally the Fool also carries a note of trust in life itself. The bag is small, the dog is eager, the sun is up. Waite called the card a spirit in search of experience, and that framing helps: you are not being told to be careless, only to accept that some things can be learned only by doing them. Look at where you have been over-preparing as a way to avoid starting. The card suggests a small, real first step often teaches more than another month of planning."],"reversed":["Reversed, the Fool can show the leap taken without looking. Perhaps a decision is being driven by boredom or by a wish to escape something, rather than by real interest in where you are heading. The bundle is packed badly, the warning of the dog is ignored, and the cliff edge is closer than it seems. This is less a verdict on spontaneity than a prompt to ask what you are running from, and whether you have left out anything essential.","The reversal can also describe the opposite problem: standing at the edge for so long that the moment passes. Fear of looking foolish can keep a gifted person frozen for years. If this applies, consider what the worst realistic outcome would be, and whether you could survive it. Sometimes the card is simply saying that a plan needs one more honest check, and sometimes it says the check has been done and the waiting is now just habit."],"love":"In love, the Fool describes the lightness of a new connection, or a decision to be open after a period of guardedness. It favors curiosity over scripts and invites you to enjoy not knowing where things go. In an established relationship it can mean bringing play and surprise back into daily life. Honest communication still matters, since enthusiasm alone does not build trust. Reversed, it may point to idealizing someone you barely know, or to avoiding commitment by treating everything as a game.","career":"At work, the Fool suggests a fresh start: a new role, a change of field, a venture with no guarantee. Your lack of fixed assumptions can be an asset, because you may notice options that specialists overlook. Treat it as encouragement to experiment on a small scale before betting everything. Ask questions, volunteer for unfamiliar tasks and accept that early mistakes are part of the learning. If you are stuck, the card questions whether your caution is protecting you or simply keeping you comfortable.","money":"Around money, the Fool points to a mindset more than a method. It can reflect willingness to try a new income stream or a different way of living, but it also warns against treating a budget as optional. Consider what you can afford to lose and what you cannot. A modest experiment that teaches you something is usually wiser than a dramatic gesture. This is reflection, not financial advice, and any serious decision deserves proper professional guidance.","advice":"As advice, the Fool says to begin, and to carry less than you think you need. Let curiosity lead for a while and be gentle with yourself when things go awkwardly. At the same time, notice the cliff: a good beginning still includes one honest look at the ground. Trust that you can adapt as you go, and treat any outcome as information rather than as a final judgment.","feelings":"As feelings, the Fool suggests someone who feels hopeful, a little giddy and perhaps unsure how to name what they feel. There may be excitement mixed with nervousness, the sense of standing on the brink of something. They may be drawn to you or to a new life direction without having thought it through. Reversed, the feeling can be anxiety, uncertainty or a reluctance to be pinned down.","yes_no":{"answer":"Yes","why":"The Fool leans toward yes when the question is about starting something new or taking a considered risk. If the question depends on careful preparation, treat the answer as a gentle yes with a caution to check the details."}},{"slug":"the-magician","name":"The Magician","number":"I","element":"Air","astrology":"Mercury","keywords_upright":["Focused will","Skill and resourcefulness","Manifestation","Clear intention","Using what you have"],"keywords_reversed":["Scattered effort","Manipulation","Untapped talent","Empty promises","Self-doubt"],"summary":"The Magician shows that you already hold the tools you need and that attention aimed with intent can change what happens next. It asks you to act deliberately rather than wait for permission.","upright":["The Magician is the card of agency. Where the Fool has not yet moved, the Magician has noticed what is on the table and decided to use it. The four tools suggest that you may already possess more resources than you give yourself credit for: skills, contacts, ideas, practical means, emotional range. Nothing about the card is mystical in the sense of waiting for luck. It is about concentration, the way a person can gather scattered energy and apply it to one clear purpose.","Traditional readings tie the card to Mercury, the messenger, so communication, persuasion and cleverness often belong here as well. A pitch, a conversation, a piece of writing or a negotiation may be where your focus pays off. The question the card puts to you is simple and sometimes uncomfortable: what do you actually intend? Clarity matters because the wand points one way. When your goal is vague, effort scatters, and when it is named, small actions begin to line up behind it."],"reversed":["Reversed, the Magician often points to energy that has no direction. You might be busy with a dozen half-started things, talented but unable to commit, or waiting for a perfect moment that never arrives. The tools are on the table but nobody is reaching for them. Sometimes the cause is self-doubt, a private conviction that you are not quite qualified, and the card gently questions that belief.","There is also a shadow reading of tricks and spin. A reversed Magician can describe someone, possibly you, using charm or half-truths to get an outcome, or being on the receiving end of a persuasive pitch that does not hold up. If a deal or an offer feels slick, ask for details and give yourself time. In a quieter sense, the card may simply say that skill is being underused because it is easier to stay in the audience than to step behind the table."],"love":"In love, the Magician describes deliberate attraction and the power of clear communication. You may be taking the initiative, expressing your interest directly or putting real effort into understanding someone. In a settled relationship, it favors conscious choices over drifting. The caution is about sincerity: charm should serve honesty. Reversed, it can suggest mixed messages, manipulation or a talented flirt who rarely follows words with action.","career":"For work, this is one of the stronger cards for initiative and craft. It suggests you have the know-how to launch a project, pitch an idea or build something visible. Gather your resources, pick a target and move. Skills in speaking, writing, design or technical work are often highlighted. If you feel overlooked, the card asks whether you have made your abilities easy for others to see. Reversed, scattered priorities and overpromising are the usual snags.","money":"The Magician suggests that income grows from applied skill and clear aims rather than hope. Consider what resources you already own, such as expertise or time, and how they might be used with more focus. Be wary of anything that promises easy returns, since sleight of hand has a place on this card. Treat this as reflection about mindset and practical habits, and seek qualified advice for real financial decisions.","advice":"As advice, the Magician tells you to choose one aim and act on it with what you have. Stop gathering permission and start using the tools already in hand. Say clearly what you want, and be honest in how you ask for it. Skill grows with practice, so a modest, concrete action today is worth more than a grand plan that stays in your head.","feelings":"As feelings, the Magician suggests someone who is engaged, intent and perhaps a little performative, wanting to make an impression or to make something happen. They may feel capable and energized, or strongly motivated toward you or a goal. Reversed, the feeling may be uncertainty hidden behind confidence, or a sense of not being able to show what they truly can do.","yes_no":{"answer":"Yes","why":"The Magician favors yes when you are willing to act with focus and honesty. The answer weakens if the situation depends on shortcuts or on someone else's unclear intentions."}},{"slug":"the-high-priestess","name":"The High Priestess","number":"II","element":"Water","astrology":"The Moon","keywords_upright":["Intuition","Hidden knowledge","Stillness","Inner voice","Mystery"],"keywords_reversed":["Ignored instincts","Secrets","Surface understanding","Disconnection","Confusion"],"summary":"The High Priestess asks you to listen before you act and to trust what you sense beneath the obvious facts. Some answers appear only when you stop pushing for them.","upright":["The High Priestess is the keeper of what is not said. Upright, she often arrives when the situation has layers that logic alone cannot reach: a feeling you cannot yet explain, a detail that does not fit, a conversation in which silence says more than words. She does not hurry to answer. The scroll is half hidden because understanding comes in its own time, and forcing it tends to spoil it.","This card encourages patience and private reflection. Keep a journal, take a quiet walk, notice your dreams, pay attention to the first small reaction your body gives when you hear a proposal. Intuition is not magic here, it is the mind processing more than you are consciously aware of. The two pillars remind you that the truth is rarely all one thing or all the other. You may be asked to hold two views at once and wait, before you decide, for the picture to settle."],"reversed":["Reversed, the High Priestess can show a person who has lost touch with their inner voice. Noise, busyness and other people's opinions drown it out, or you keep talking yourself out of a quiet certainty because it lacks evidence. You may feel strangely confused about what you really want, as if you have been living on the surface of your own life.","The reversal can also indicate secrets, whether kept by you or by someone close. Information that is withheld, a half-told story, a hidden agenda: the veil is thick and the curtains are drawn. This does not prove deceit, but it does suggest that things are not what they seem. Before acting, find a calm moment to ask what you already suspect, and gather facts gently rather than making accusations on a feeling alone."],"love":"In love, the High Priestess points to depth beneath the surface: a connection that is felt before it is explained, or a relationship in which much remains unspoken. It favors patience and attentive listening over pursuit. She may indicate a reserved person who reveals themselves slowly. Reversed, it suggests secrets, mixed signals or ignoring a gut feeling about someone because the story on paper looks right.","career":"At work, the card values observation over noise. It suggests you may do your best thinking by waiting, researching and noticing the unspoken dynamics in a team before moving. Fields tied to research, counseling, writing or any quiet, reflective craft are often supported. If a decision feels unclear, give it time rather than forcing a deadline. Reversed, it can describe office politics, missing information or doubts you have not yet voiced.","money":"The High Priestess advises caution around money matters that are unclear. Read the details, ask questions and be wary of anything where key information is withheld. A quiet review of your habits may reveal what a busy mind has missed. She encourages patience over impulsive moves. This is a reflective prompt only and does not replace professional financial advice.","advice":"As advice, the High Priestess says to pause and listen. Give yourself quiet, avoid rushing to conclusions and trust the small signals you have been dismissing. Do not announce everything; some things grow better kept private for now. When you do act, let your decision rest on both what you know and what you sense, not on one alone.","feelings":"As feelings, she suggests someone who feels a lot and shows little. There may be strong emotion beneath a calm exterior, or a person who is still working out what they feel and prefers not to speak until they know. Reversed, the feelings may be hidden, denied or confused even to the person who has them.","yes_no":{"answer":"Maybe","why":"The High Priestess usually means the answer is not yet fully visible. Wait, gather quiet information and revisit the question when more has been revealed."}},{"slug":"the-empress","name":"The Empress","number":"III","element":"Earth","astrology":"Venus","keywords_upright":["Abundance","Nurturing","Creativity","Sensual pleasure","Growth"],"keywords_reversed":["Smothering","Creative block","Neglecting yourself","Dependence","Stagnation"],"summary":"The Empress is the card of growth, comfort and creative fertility. She asks you to nourish yourself and what you care about, and to let things ripen at their own pace.","upright":["The Empress stands for the creative, nurturing force that makes things grow, whether that is a family, a garden, a business or a body of work. Upright, she asks you to pay attention to the physical and the sensory: food, rest, touch, beauty, time outdoors. There is nothing frivolous in this. A well-fed, well-rested person tends to think and love better, and the card honors that quiet fact.","She also speaks of abundance, though in a grounded sense. Things ripen when conditions are right and attention is given steadily. This may be a season to tend what you have started, to host, to make, to care, rather than to chase something new. Her pregnancy-linked symbolism can mean a literal arrival, but more often it points to a project or a feeling that is taking shape. Warmth and generosity are the keynote, and it helps to remember that you also deserve some of the care you give away."],"reversed":["Reversed, the Empress can describe nurturing turned lopsided. You may be giving so much to others that your own needs have thinned out, or you may be resting so long in comfort that nothing moves. A creative project may stall, and the pleasant garden may go to seed because no one tends it.","The reversal can also point to smothering, where care becomes control and love tries to manage the other person's choices. If this resonates, ask whether your help is being asked for. Sometimes the card is simply asking you to return to your body and your basic routines, such as sleep, food and rest, before expecting inspiration to arrive. Growth cannot be forced, but it can be neglected."],"love":"In love, the Empress suggests warmth, affection, sensuality and a relationship that feels like home. It favors generosity, shared meals, physical closeness and the pleasure of caring for someone without keeping score. In some readings it points to family-building or deepening commitment. Reversed, it can show over-giving, jealousy or a partnership that has become too comfortable to grow.","career":"At work, the Empress favors creative, caring and growth-oriented efforts. Fields involving hospitality, design, education, health, food or any kind of cultivating are often highlighted. She suggests patient building, supportive team dynamics and results that develop steadily. Pay attention to the environment you work in, since beauty and comfort feed productivity. Reversed, she can describe burnout from overcaring or a creative drought that needs real rest.","money":"Around money, the Empress points to steady, organic growth and a healthy relationship with comfort. She invites you to consider what makes you feel secure and what you value enough to spend on. Generosity is welcome, but so is looking after your own base. Treat the card as a prompt for reflection about habits and values, and consult a qualified professional for financial decisions.","advice":"As advice, the Empress says to nourish yourself and what you love. Slow down enough to enjoy something, spend time in nature and give your projects regular, patient attention. Create something with your hands. Let abundance be built from small acts of care rather than from pressure. And allow yourself to receive as well as give.","feelings":"As feelings, the Empress suggests someone who feels affectionate, protective and warmly engaged. They may want to take care of you, or to be cared for. There may be contentment, sensual enjoyment or a wish to settle and build. Reversed, the feeling can be possessiveness, restlessness inside comfort or exhaustion from caring.","yes_no":{"answer":"Yes","why":"The Empress leans toward yes for questions about growth, creativity, comfort and relationships. Expect results to arrive gradually, not instantly."}},{"slug":"the-emperor","name":"The Emperor","number":"IV","element":"Fire","astrology":"Aries","keywords_upright":["Structure","Authority","Stability","Leadership","Protection"],"keywords_reversed":["Rigidity","Domination","Disorder","Abuse of power","Immaturity"],"summary":"The Emperor stands for order, boundaries and responsible authority. He asks you to build something stable and to lead with discipline rather than impulse.","upright":["The Emperor is structure given a face. Where the Empress nourishes, he organizes: laws, schedules, foundations, boundaries. Upright, the card often appears when a situation calls for firm decisions, a clear plan or a steadier hand. You may need to be the one who takes responsibility, sets expectations and sees a commitment through even when enthusiasm has worn off.","At its best, this is the authority that protects. A good leader creates conditions in which others can thrive, and a good boundary is a form of respect. The card may point to a father figure, a mentor, a boss, or to the part of yourself that insists on discipline. It asks what you are building and whether the framework you have in place can hold weight. Rules and routines may feel dull compared with inspiration, but they are what let ambition last beyond the first week."],"reversed":["Reversed, the Emperor can become the tyrant: control used to calm one's own fear, orders given without listening, rules that exist to protect ego. If you recognize this in a person around you, it may be worth looking at how much space you are allowed. If it is yours, ask what you are afraid would happen if you loosened your grip.","The reversal can also show the opposite failure, a lack of structure. Plans collapse, responsibilities slide and no one is quite in charge. You may find it difficult to hold a line, keep a promise to yourself or accept authority you do not agree with. In both directions the question is about healthy power: who holds it, how it is used and whether it serves something larger than itself."],"love":"In love, the Emperor suggests commitment, reliability and a partner who offers security. It values clear agreements over vague hopes and shows up when stability is more important than excitement. It may describe a protective, perhaps traditional person. Reversed, it points to controlling behavior, emotional rigidity or a relationship in which one person sets all the rules.","career":"In work matters, the Emperor favors leadership, organization and long-term planning. It suggests that you may be stepping into responsibility, establishing systems or being taken seriously for your competence. Clear goals, defined roles and steady discipline are your allies. This is a good moment to formalize a plan. Reversed, it may describe an overbearing manager, power struggles or a business without proper foundations.","money":"The Emperor encourages order in finances: clear records, realistic limits and long-term thinking. It suggests that security comes from structure and consistent habits rather than from luck. Consider where your money practices are loose and where they are overly rigid. This is reflection on mindset only, and anything significant calls for qualified professional advice.","advice":"As advice, the Emperor says to build a structure and keep to it. Set clear boundaries, make a plan with dates attached and take responsibility for your part. Lead with steadiness instead of force, and remember that authority earns respect by being fair. If a rule is no longer serving you, change it deliberately rather than abandoning it in a mood.","feelings":"As feelings, the Emperor suggests someone who feels responsible, protective and perhaps guarded. Emotion may be expressed through action, providing and organizing, rather than through words. There may be a sense of duty, pride or a wish to be respected. Reversed, the feelings can be defensiveness, anger at losing control or difficulty showing vulnerability.","yes_no":{"answer":"Yes","why":"The Emperor tends toward yes when a plan is realistic, organized and backed by commitment. It becomes a no if the answer depends on bending rules or controlling other people."}},{"slug":"the-hierophant","name":"The Hierophant","number":"V","element":"Earth","astrology":"Taurus","keywords_upright":["Tradition","Shared beliefs","Mentorship","Institutions","Commitment to a path"],"keywords_reversed":["Rebellion","Questioning dogma","Personal approach","Restriction","Hollow conformity"],"summary":"The Hierophant speaks for tradition, teaching and the wisdom held by communities. It asks whether a proven path can support you, or whether it is time to question it.","upright":["The Hierophant is the card of established forms: schools, faiths, professions, families, ceremonies. Upright, it often points to a situation in which learning from those who came before is more useful than reinventing everything. You might be studying under a mentor, entering an institution or taking part in a ritual that links you to a larger group. Many people find real comfort and a sense of belonging in such frameworks.","The card also speaks of shared values. Think about the beliefs you inherited or chose, and how they shape your decisions. Sometimes the card advises following the conventional route because it works: completing the qualification, respecting the process, keeping a promise made in public. It can also recommend seeking a teacher. The challenge is to be a student without losing your own judgment, taking what is sound and leaving the rest."],"reversed":["Reversed, the Hierophant often marks a questioning of the rules. A belief you were given no longer fits, a role feels like a costume, an institution has let you down. This can be liberating, an honest step toward a personal philosophy, though it may bring friction with family, colleagues or community who prefer things as they were.","The reversal can also warn against empty conformity, doing things a certain way only because they have always been done so, or because you fear what others would say. On the other hand, it can show rebellion for its own sake, where rejecting everything becomes another form of dependence on the thing rejected. The useful question is what you would keep if you were choosing freely, and what you would let go."],"love":"In love, the Hierophant leans toward commitment, shared values and traditional milestones such as engagement, marriage or meeting the family. It suggests a relationship supported by common beliefs and mutual respect for each other's backgrounds. Reversed, it may point to dissatisfaction with expected roles, unconventional arrangements or tension over differing values.","career":"At work, the card favors established paths: qualifications, mentorship, institutions and following procedure. It may signal advancement through recognized channels or a role in teaching and guiding others. Learning the rules thoroughly gives you the right to adapt them later. Reversed, it suggests restlessness inside a rigid organization, or a wish to work in a way that does not follow the standard model.","money":"Around money, the Hierophant points to proven, conventional approaches over novelty. Consider what financial habits you inherited from your family and whether they still serve you. Advice from an experienced, qualified source may be especially relevant. This is reflection on values and mindset only, not a recommendation for any particular financial decision.","advice":"As advice, the Hierophant says to learn from those with experience and to respect the process. Find a teacher, join a community or follow a proven method for now. Keep your own conscience engaged while doing so. Where a tradition truly supports you, lean on it, and where it does not, be honest with yourself about that.","feelings":"As feelings, the Hierophant suggests someone who feels a strong sense of duty, loyalty or a wish to do things properly. They may value stability and shared standards, and may find it hard to express feelings outside accepted forms. Reversed, the feeling can be impatience with expectations or a longing for greater freedom.","yes_no":{"answer":"Maybe","why":"The Hierophant says yes if the matter fits established norms and shared values, and leans toward no if it requires breaking from them. The answer depends on whether the conventional path suits you."}},{"slug":"the-lovers","name":"The Lovers","number":"VI","element":"Air","astrology":"Gemini","keywords_upright":["Choice and alignment","Deep connection","Shared values","Union","Honesty"],"keywords_reversed":["Misalignment","Indecision","Disharmony","Avoided choice","Imbalance"],"summary":"The Lovers is as much about choosing as about romance. It asks you to act in line with your values and to meet others openly and honestly.","upright":["The Lovers is often called the card of relationship, but its older meaning is choice. The figures in the garden are standing in front of a decision that will shape who they become, and the angel above suggests that the choice is made best when heart and conscience agree. Upright, the card often appears when you face a significant fork and need to decide according to what you actually value rather than what is convenient.","Romantic connection is certainly part of the picture: attraction, intimacy, honest partnership. But the same principle applies in friendship and work. A genuine bond is one in which you can be seen as you are. The card asks you to examine alignment between what you say you believe and what you do. When these match, there is a sense of wholeness. When they do not, the discomfort is information, and the card invites you to listen to it."],"reversed":["Reversed, The Lovers can point to values in conflict, relationships out of balance or an important decision postponed until it is made for you. You may be drawn toward something that you know does not fit, or you may be going along with a choice because refusing it feels awkward. The angel is still there, but the figures are no longer looking up.","The reversal can also describe a rift: a misunderstanding, a loss of trust or simply two people who have grown in different directions. It does not always signal an ending. Often it is a prompt to talk plainly about what each person wants, and to face the choice you have been avoiding. Indecision is itself a decision, and it is worth asking what it is costing you."],"love":"In love, The Lovers speaks of deep connection, mutual attraction and relationships built on honest values. It can mark a significant bond or a conscious decision to commit. It also asks both people to be present and truthful. Reversed, it may suggest temptation, imbalance, differing values or a choice between people or paths that has not been faced.","career":"At work, the card often marks an important decision: a choice between roles, directions or partners. Let your values weigh heavily in it. It can also point to a productive collaboration with someone whose goals match yours. Ask whether your job supports the life you want. Reversed, it may suggest conflicting loyalties, a mismatch with the organization or a decision you keep delaying.","money":"In money matters, The Lovers invites you to look at how your spending and saving reflect your values. Shared finances or joint decisions may be in focus, and honesty between those involved is essential. Consider what a choice would cost beyond the price tag. This is reflection only, and major financial decisions deserve qualified professional guidance.","advice":"As advice, The Lovers says to choose in line with your values and to be honest about what you want. Speak clearly to those involved and avoid deciding by default. Notice where your actions and your beliefs part company, and close that gap. A choice made with your whole self tends to feel steadier afterward, even when it is difficult.","feelings":"As feelings, The Lovers suggests someone who feels strongly drawn, emotionally open and invested in the connection. They may be weighing a choice or feeling torn between desire and duty. Reversed, the feelings may be conflicted, distant or confused by competing wants.","yes_no":{"answer":"Yes","why":"The Lovers leans yes when the choice matches your values and you can be honest about it. If you feel torn, the answer is less about the card than about clarifying what you truly want."}},{"slug":"the-chariot","name":"The Chariot","number":"VII","element":"Water","astrology":"Cancer","keywords_upright":["Determination","Self-mastery","Forward momentum","Victory through effort","Discipline"],"keywords_reversed":["Loss of direction","Aggression","Lack of control","Stalling","Forcing outcomes"],"summary":"The Chariot shows progress won by disciplined will and clear direction. It asks you to steer opposing forces in yourself toward one goal.","upright":["The Chariot is the card of directed momentum. The charioteer holds no reins, which is the key to the image: he steers through concentration, not through brute force. The two sphinxes stand for opposing tendencies, such as desire and duty, caution and ambition, heart and head. Upright, the card suggests that you can move forward by bringing those forces into alignment behind one clear intention.","Traditionally it is a card of victory, but of the earned kind. It often appears when success depends on discipline, travel, determination or a willingness to endure friction without losing your course. It may refer to a physical journey, a project push or an emotional effort to keep going. Ask what exactly you are steering toward. Momentum without a destination is simply speed, and the card gains its power from a goal that has been chosen clearly and held steadily."],"reversed":["Reversed, The Chariot suggests the sphinxes are pulling apart. Competing priorities leave you spinning or standing still, and you may feel that life is steering you instead of the other way around. The remedy is rarely more force. It is usually a pause to decide what matters most and what can be let go for now.","The reversal can also describe aggression, pushing through at the cost of others, or insisting on winning when the situation calls for cooperation. Sometimes it shows a stalled journey: delays, lost direction or a lack of drive. A careful look at what you are trying to control and what you have lost sight of can help you take the reins back, or recognize that the road needs a different plan."],"love":"In love, The Chariot suggests moving a relationship forward through shared effort and clear intention. It can describe a partner who is driven and determined, or a relationship that has to overcome obstacles together. Self-discipline within a partnership matters. Reversed, it may point to conflict about direction, controlling behavior or a relationship that has stalled because no one is steering.","career":"At work, the Chariot is a strong card for ambition, focus and visible progress. It supports a push toward a goal, a competition, a promotion or a demanding project. Sort out conflicting demands and commit to one plan. Travel or commuting may also feature. Reversed, it suggests losing direction, burnout from overdriving or clashes with colleagues over who leads.","money":"Around money, the Chariot points to disciplined, goal-directed effort. Defining a concrete target and sticking to a plan may help. Be careful about aggressive moves driven by the wish to win. Consider whether competing desires are pulling your spending in opposite directions. This is reflection only and not a substitute for qualified financial advice.","advice":"As advice, the Chariot says to pick a direction and commit. Bring your conflicting impulses into line, then move with steady, disciplined effort. Do not confuse force with control, since steady focus is stronger than pressure. Stay open to correcting the course without abandoning the destination.","feelings":"As feelings, the Chariot suggests someone who feels determined, driven and focused, and perhaps tightly self-controlled. They may be intent on a goal and less available emotionally. Reversed, the feeling may be frustration, overwhelm or a sense of lacking control over events.","yes_no":{"answer":"Yes","why":"The Chariot leans yes when you are willing to commit effort and keep a clear goal. It is a conditional yes: progress depends on your own discipline."}},{"slug":"strength","name":"Strength","number":"VIII","element":"Fire","astrology":"Leo","keywords_upright":["Quiet courage","Compassion","Patience","Inner strength","Gentle mastery"],"keywords_reversed":["Self-doubt","Raw emotion","Insecurity","Forced control","Depleted energy"],"summary":"Strength shows that real power lies in patience, compassion and steady courage rather than force. It asks you to meet your own fierce side with kindness.","upright":["Strength is a card about the kind of courage that does not announce itself. The woman does not wrestle the lion. She rests her hands on it, and the animal yields. In everyday life this might be the patience to stay with a hard conversation, to remain calm in front of someone's anger or to keep trying after a setback without hardening. The card honors endurance and gentleness together.","The lion often stands for your own instincts: anger, appetite, fear, ambition. Upright, the card suggests that these are not enemies to be crushed but energies to understand and direct. Suppressing them tends to make them louder. Befriending them lets their force be used well. If you have been harshly critical of yourself, the card invites a softer approach. Confidence of this kind grows from self-acceptance, and it often turns out to be more persuasive than any display of dominance."],"reversed":["Reversed, Strength can show a loss of confidence, the sense that the lion is in charge. Raw feelings may break through, such as anger, panic or craving, or you may be running on fumes and doubting that you have anything left to give. This is not weakness in character. It is often a sign that you have carried a lot for a long time.","The reversal can also describe forced control: clenching hard to keep a feeling down, or using pressure on others to make them behave. Neither works for long. The card suggests going back to basics, rest, honest acknowledgment of the emotion and small steps toward steadiness. Courage can be rebuilt, and asking for support is often part of how that happens."],"love":"In love, Strength suggests a relationship tempered by patience, compassion and emotional resilience. It encourages gentle handling of conflict, and a willingness to see the vulnerable side of a partner. It can indicate a bond that has weathered difficulty. Reversed, it may point to insecurity, explosive reactions or one person suppressing their needs to keep the peace.","career":"At work, Strength favors calm leadership, resilience under pressure and persuasion by example. It suggests handling a difficult colleague or situation with composure instead of confrontation. Perseverance through a demanding project may be rewarded. Reversed, it can describe loss of confidence, burnout or a team in which pressure has replaced trust.","money":"In financial matters, Strength points to steady discipline and keeping calm under pressure instead of reacting to fear or desire. Consider what impulses, such as comfort spending or anxiety, are driving your decisions. Patience tends to serve better than force. This is a mindset reflection only, and serious decisions merit professional advice.","advice":"As advice, Strength says to meet the difficulty with patience and warmth. Treat your own strong feelings as something to understand, not to scold. Take small, steady steps and stay calm when pressure rises. You do not need to prove anything by force, and gentleness often accomplishes what loudness cannot.","feelings":"As feelings, Strength suggests someone who feels quietly confident, caring and emotionally steady, or who is working hard to stay composed. They may be protective and patient with you. Reversed, the feelings may be fragile, overwhelmed or conflicted behind a controlled front.","yes_no":{"answer":"Yes","why":"Strength leans yes when the situation can be handled with patience and steady courage. It suggests a good outcome comes from calm persistence, not from pressure."}},{"slug":"the-hermit","name":"The Hermit","number":"IX","element":"Earth","astrology":"Virgo","keywords_upright":["Solitude","Inner guidance","Reflection","Wisdom","Searching within"],"keywords_reversed":["Isolation","Loneliness","Withdrawal","Refusing guidance","Lost direction"],"summary":"The Hermit invites you to step back from the noise and look inward for guidance. It values solitude as a place to think, not as an escape.","upright":["The Hermit asks you to withdraw, at least for a while. The world is loud and full of opinions, and some questions cannot be answered until you step away from it. Upright, the card often appears when you need time alone to sort out what you truly think: after a loss, before a big decision, when you have lost track of your own preferences. It is a respectable kind of solitude, chosen, purposeful and temporary.","The lantern is a key detail. It does not light the whole mountain, only the way ahead, and the star inside suggests wisdom that has been earned. The Hermit is also a teacher in the tradition, someone who has climbed higher and now holds a light for those below. You might find that a mentor, a book or a quiet period of study gives you what you need. Do not expect immediate results. The card favors slow, careful understanding over quick conclusions."],"reversed":["Reversed, the Hermit can show solitude gone sour. Time alone becomes isolation, and withdrawal becomes a way to avoid people, feelings or responsibilities. You may be feeling lonely and not know how to reach out, or you may have convinced yourself that nobody could understand.","The reversal can also point to the opposite problem: a refusal to be alone with yourself. Constant company, noise or activity can keep reflection at bay. If so, the card suggests small, manageable doses of quiet. In either direction, the lantern needs tending. Reconnecting with trusted people, or finally making space to think, might be the way back to a clear path."],"love":"In love, the Hermit suggests a period of introspection about what you want and need from a relationship. It may describe someone who is private and slow to open, or a single person who is happily self-contained for now. In a partnership, it asks for respect for each other's need for space. Reversed, it can mean loneliness, emotional withdrawal or fear of intimacy.","career":"At work, the Hermit favors focused, independent effort: research, study, writing, craft or reflection on your direction. You may benefit from stepping away from distractions or from consulting a seasoned mentor. It can also suggest a career pause to reassess. Reversed, it may indicate isolation at work, refusing help or avoiding feedback.","money":"Around money, the Hermit encourages a quiet, careful review of your finances and priorities. Consider what you truly need and what you spend to fill other gaps. Patience and thoughtful research serve better than impulse. This is reflection only, and any significant financial choice deserves qualified professional advice.","advice":"As advice, the Hermit says to make time for solitude and to listen to yourself there. Turn down the noise, take a walk, write or study without interruption. Seek out wise counsel if you need it, and trust the small light you have. Come back to others when you are ready, bringing what you found.","feelings":"As feelings, the Hermit suggests someone who feels thoughtful, introspective and in need of quiet. They may be processing deeply, or stepping back to find clarity, not rejecting you. Reversed, the feelings may be loneliness, shyness or a wish to disappear.","yes_no":{"answer":"Maybe","why":"The Hermit usually says the answer is not ready to be found by asking others. Take time alone, think it through and return to the question when you feel clearer."}},{"slug":"wheel-of-fortune","name":"Wheel of Fortune","number":"X","element":"Fire","astrology":"Jupiter","keywords_upright":["Cycles and change","Turning point","Fortune","Fate and timing","Movement"],"keywords_reversed":["Resistance to change","Bad timing","Setback","Loss of control","Repeating patterns"],"summary":"The Wheel of Fortune reminds you that circumstances turn, for better and for worse. It asks you to move with change while staying anchored in your own choices.","upright":["The Wheel of Fortune is the card of cycles. Seasons change, tides turn and a period of difficulty or luck eventually shifts into something else. Upright, it often shows up when the winds are changing: an unexpected opportunity, a sudden turn of events or the sense that timing is working with you. The card invites you to notice where you are on the wheel, and not to assume that either the high or the low is permanent.","The figures around the wheel hold books, a reminder that the pattern can be studied. Waite described the card as fortune, but also as the order behind apparent chance. This does not mean everything is fixed. It means that some things are beyond your control, while your response to them is not. When change arrives, flexibility is a strength. Ask which parts of the situation you can influence and which you can only meet with grace. The sphinx on top, calm and armed, hints at a steady mind at the center of movement."],"reversed":["Reversed, the Wheel can signal a stretch where things seem to go against you. A plan stalls, timing is off or a familiar pattern keeps repeating. It can feel as though you have no say. The card does not call this permanent, since wheels keep turning, but it does suggest that fighting the moment may be less useful than waiting and preparing.","The reversal can also show resistance to a change that is trying to happen. You may be clinging to the way things used to be, or refusing to see a pattern in your own choices. Sometimes the card points to what you keep repeating. If a story keeps coming back in different clothes, ask what part you might be playing. Change often begins when you alter one small habit instead of waiting for luck."],"love":"In love, the Wheel of Fortune suggests a turning point: a meeting that feels timely, a shift in a relationship or a change that leads somewhere new. It reminds you that feelings and circumstances move in cycles. Staying flexible helps. Reversed, it may indicate repeating patterns, poor timing or resistance to a change a relationship needs.","career":"At work, the Wheel points to shifting circumstances: a new opening, a change of role, an unexpected development or a cycle ending. Adaptability and awareness of timing are valuable now. It encourages you to make use of opportunities when they appear. Reversed, it can describe a setback, restructuring or a pattern in your work life that keeps repeating.","money":"In money matters, the Wheel of Fortune reflects the natural ups and downs of income and expense. It suggests keeping a longer view and preparing for changes in both directions. Luck may play a part, but habits matter more. This is mindset reflection only, and decisions about money deserve qualified professional advice.","advice":"As advice, the Wheel says to stay flexible and keep the long view. Accept that some things are changing regardless of your preference, and focus on your response. Use opportunities while they are present, and do not take a good stretch for granted. If you are in a low, remember that it moves.","feelings":"As feelings, the Wheel suggests someone who feels that things are shifting, perhaps with a mix of hope and uncertainty. They may feel carried by events, or that a connection is fated. Reversed, the feeling may be frustration at bad timing or a sense of being stuck in the same pattern.","yes_no":{"answer":"Yes","why":"The Wheel of Fortune leans yes because it signals movement and a favorable turn. It is a moderate yes, since timing and events outside your control play a part."}},{"slug":"justice","name":"Justice","number":"XI","element":"Air","astrology":"Libra","keywords_upright":["Fairness and balance","Accountability","Clear-eyed truth","Cause and effect","Honest decisions"],"keywords_reversed":["Bias or unfairness","Avoiding responsibility","Dishonesty","Unresolved consequences","Skewed judgment"],"summary":"Justice asks you to look at a situation as it actually is and to weigh it without favoritism, including toward yourself. What you have set in motion returns in proportion, and clarity now saves confusion later.","upright":["Justice arrives when a matter calls for an honest accounting. It speaks of decisions that carry weight: contracts, agreements, apologies, choices about who gets what and who owes what. Where other cards lean on emotion or intuition, this one leans on reason and evidence. Waite linked it with equity and the balanced outcome that follows right action, and that framing still helps. Whatever has been done is now being measured, and the measurement is rarely cruel. It is simply accurate.\n\nThe card also points inward. Before you judge anyone else, it asks whether you have been fair with yourself, and whether your story of a situation would survive being read aloud to a neutral listener. Sometimes the result is relief, because careful honesty clears away guilt that was never earned. Sometimes it means admitting a share of responsibility. Either way, the energy here rewards those who gather facts, listen to both sides and act in a way they could explain without flinching.","There is a patient quality in Justice. It does not rush toward a verdict, and it does not bend because someone is persuasive. In a reading it often shows that a slow, procedural process is under way, such as paperwork, a negotiation or a long conversation that finally gets to the point. The tarot does not promise which way it falls. It says the process will be governed by what is real, not by what is wished for.\n\nThis can be a steadying card if you have been wronged and are worried the truth will be buried. It can also be a sober one if you have been coasting on charm or half-truths. The practical invitation is to keep records, say what you mean plainly, and decide from principle rather than from the mood of the moment. Balance here is not a compromise made to keep the peace. It is the point where each side of a question has been given its due weight."],"reversed":["Reversed, Justice suggests the scales are tilted, or that you are unwilling to look at them. This might show up as bias in your own thinking: a conclusion reached first and evidence collected afterward. It can also describe a situation in which rules are applied unevenly, someone is shifting blame, or a promised fairness never quite arrives. The frustration is real, and the card does not ask you to pretend otherwise.\n\nIt can also point to dishonesty, whether given or received. Facts may be missing, a version of events may have been polished, or you may be avoiding an uncomfortable detail because acknowledging it would require action. The reversal rarely means that justice is impossible. It means the process is stalled or distorted, and clearer information is needed before anything can settle.","Another reading of the reversed card concerns consequences you are trying to outrun. An unpaid debt of any kind, an unmade apology or an unfinished commitment tends to keep its weight until it is addressed. Delay does not cancel it. If this card falls in that position, consider what you have been postponing and whether facing it now would cost less than facing it later.\n\nIt may equally describe harshness: judging yourself or another person far more severely than the facts warrant. Perfectionism can pass for fairness while being nothing of the sort. The invitation is to restore proportion, to check your sources, and to let mercy sit alongside accuracy instead of being treated as its opposite."],"love":"In relationships, Justice asks for fairness and honest dealing. It often appears when two people are taking stock: who carries more of the load, what was agreed, and whether the connection rests on truth. It can support a clean conversation that clears the air, or a commitment made after clear thought rather than momentum. If you are single, it suggests choosing based on values and observed behavior, not chemistry alone. Reversed, it points to imbalance, unspoken scorekeeping or a refusal to own your part in a pattern.","career":"At work, Justice favors contracts, evaluations, legal or compliance matters and any decision that must be defensible. It can indicate a fair hearing, a promotion or review that reflects real performance, or the need to document your efforts. It rewards preparation and straight communication over office politics. If you are hiring, negotiating or resolving a dispute, aim for clear criteria. Reversed, it may mean favoritism, unclear expectations or a decision that feels arbitrary, which calls for calm, factual follow-up.","money":"Financially, Justice reflects accounts that should balance: what comes in against what goes out, what is owed, what is earned. It suggests reading the terms, keeping tidy records and acting with integrity in shared money matters. Taxes, settlements and agreements may be in focus. Reversed, it points to avoided statements or unclear arrangements. The card encourages clarity and, where stakes are high, qualified professional advice.","advice":"As advice, Justice says to get the facts straight and decide from principle. Write down what you know, separate it from what you assume, and listen to the side you find least comfortable. Then act in a way that you could explain plainly to anyone affected. Own what is yours, and refuse what is not. Fairness is a practice, and it starts with how you speak to yourself.","feelings":"As feelings, Justice describes someone who is thinking carefully and trying to be fair. They may be weighing the situation rather than reacting, which can look cool from the outside. Their regard is likely measured and based on how you actually behave. If the card is reversed, their view may be clouded by resentment, or they may be holding back an honest conversation they know is due.","yes_no":{"answer":"Maybe","why":"Justice leans toward whatever is fair and earned, so the answer depends on whether the facts support it. Check the evidence honestly: if your case is sound, the answer tilts toward yes."}},{"slug":"the-hanged-man","name":"The Hanged Man","number":"XII","element":"Water","astrology":"Neptune","keywords_upright":["Pause and surrender","New perspective","Letting go of control","Voluntary sacrifice","Suspended time"],"keywords_reversed":["Stalling without purpose","Resisting the pause","Needless martyrdom","Fear of change","Missing the lesson"],"summary":"The Hanged Man marks a deliberate pause in which pushing harder does nothing and seeing differently does everything. Release the need to force an outcome, and an unexpected angle may appear.","upright":["The Hanged Man shows up when forward motion has stalled and effort no longer helps. Instead of treating that as failure, the card frames it as a pause with a purpose. Something is being reorganized beneath the surface, and your task is to stop interfering. Many people meet this card during a waiting period: an unanswered application, a relationship at a standstill, a decision that cannot yet be made. The usual instinct is to act anyway. This card says to rest in the uncertainty long enough for it to show you something.\n\nThere is also an element of willing surrender. The figure is not trapped, and the gentle face tells us he has accepted the situation. In practical terms, that means releasing a rigid plan or a fixed opinion. You may have to give something up, whether time, certainty or a preferred outcome, to receive an insight you could not reach by pushing. The reward is perspective, which arrives quietly and often changes what you were trying to do in the first place.","This card also invites you to turn a problem upside down. If you have approached a matter from one direction for weeks, try the opposite: argue the other person's case, imagine the goal already achieved, ask what the situation is trying to teach. Neptune, the traditional planet attached to the card, carries a dissolving, dreamlike quality, and the Hanged Man works by loosening hard edges so that other options can surface.\n\nIt need not be passive. A pause can include reading, journaling, conversations and observation. What it lacks is the forcing. If you feel restless, notice that restlessness without obeying it. When the suspension ends, it usually ends on its own, and you tend to move with a clearer sense of what matters. The lesson of the card is that surrender, chosen consciously, is a form of strength and not of weakness."],"reversed":["Reversed, the Hanged Man often points to a pause that has lost its meaning. You may be stuck in indecision, delaying because commitment feels risky, or waiting for permission that nobody is going to give. The suspension that was once useful has become a hiding place. The card asks whether you are really learning something or simply avoiding the discomfort of choosing.\n\nIt can also describe resistance to a pause that is needed. Perhaps you keep pushing, filling your calendar and refusing to rest because stillness brings up feelings you would rather not meet. In that case the reversal is a mild warning that forcing the pace will not produce the outcome you want, and the tension may come out sideways, through fatigue or friction.","A second theme concerns sacrifice. Reversed, the card can show someone giving up far more than is fair and calling it patience, or playing the martyr in a situation that does not require it. If you are enduring something and nothing is being gained, ask honestly what you are waiting for and who benefits from your waiting.\n\nIt may also signal a missed lesson, where a stalled period passes without reflection and the same pattern returns. The remedy is not dramatic. Name what you have been avoiding, choose one small action that tests the situation, and allow yourself to move. Waiting is wise when it is deliberate. Otherwise it is just delay wearing a calmer expression."],"love":"In love, the Hanged Man describes a suspended stage: a connection on hold, an unclear status, or a period of reassessment in which neither person is ready to move. It suggests patience and a willingness to see the other person's point of view without needing to win. Giving up an old expectation may make room for something more honest. Reversed, it points to staying in limbo out of fear, or making sacrifices that go unrecognized and unbalanced.","career":"Professionally, the card suggests a plateau, a project on hold or a role that is shifting in ways you cannot yet see. Instead of forcing a result, use the pause to gather information, reconsider your approach and listen to colleagues. A different angle on a stuck problem may be the real breakthrough. Reversed, it can indicate procrastination, an unwillingness to commit to a direction, or staying overlong in a situation that no longer fits.","money":"Financially, the Hanged Man advises holding steady rather than making impulsive moves. It may show delays, a slow period or a need to rethink a strategy before committing. A short-term sacrifice can serve a longer aim. Reversed, it points to indecision that costs opportunity, or sacrificing without purpose. Take time to review the numbers and consult qualified help for large decisions.","advice":"As advice, the Hanged Man says to stop forcing and start observing. Put down the plan for a while and see what shows up when you are not pushing. Look at the problem from the opposite side. Let yourself not know the answer yet. If the pause feels uncomfortable, that is part of the process. Set a gentle limit, then check again whether you are ready to move.","feelings":"As feelings, the Hanged Man suggests someone who is in a reflective, uncertain place. They may be unsure what they want, thinking things over, or holding back until something becomes clear. It is not necessarily disinterest. They may be seeing things from a new angle. Reversed, they could be avoiding a decision or hesitant to admit how they feel.","yes_no":{"answer":"Maybe","why":"The Hanged Man says the answer is not available yet. Wait, gather perspective and revisit the question once the situation has had time to settle."}},{"slug":"death","name":"Death","number":"XIII","element":"Water","astrology":"Scorpio","keywords_upright":["Endings and transformation","Letting go","Clearing the old","Necessary change","Rebirth"],"keywords_reversed":["Resisting change","Clinging to the past","Stagnation","Fear of endings","Slow transition"],"summary":"Death is the card of endings that make room for something new. It is almost never about physical death and almost always about a chapter, habit or identity that has run its course.","upright":["Death is probably the most misunderstood card in the deck, and the first thing to say is that it rarely predicts anyone's physical death. It is the card of transition. Something has finished, or is finishing, and the old form cannot be kept alive. That might be a job, a belief, a friendship, a stage of life, an identity you have outgrown. The skeleton does not negotiate, and neither does time. The image reminds us that even kings fall, and that endings arrive whether or not we feel ready.\n\nWhat the card offers is honesty about what is already over. Often the hardest part is not the loss itself but the long period of pretending that the loss has not happened. When Death appears, you are invited to acknowledge it, grieve what needs grieving, and stop spending energy on something that has no life left. The banner with its white rose suggests that something living grows from this clearing, even if you cannot see it yet.","A reading with Death in it often carries relief mixed with sadness. The two can sit together. You may feel the pull of what you are leaving and, at the same time, a quiet sense that this ending is correct. In the Golden Dawn tradition the card belongs to Scorpio and to the element of water, which suits its themes of deep change, the breaking down of the surface so that something truer can emerge.\n\nPractically, it can help to ask what is asking to be released and what is ready to begin. Endings are easier when they are marked: a clean-out, a conversation, a small ritual, a decision made out loud. Death does not demand drama. It asks for willingness. Once the old form is put down, space opens, and the next chapter can start from fresh ground instead of being built on top of something that no longer holds."],"reversed":["Reversed, Death usually describes resistance to an ending that is already under way. You may be holding on to a situation, a role or a relationship long past its natural close, hoping that enough effort will bring it back to life. The tension of that resistance can show up as stagnation, a sense of being stuck, or recurring problems that keep returning in different clothes. Nothing is wrong with grief. The difficulty comes from refusing to let the change complete.\n\nThe reversal can also point to a transformation that is slow or partial. Things have shifted, but not fully, and you are standing between the old and the new. This is often uncomfortable, and the temptation is to retreat to the familiar. The card suggests being patient with an unfinished transition while still taking small steps toward it.","Fear is a common companion of the reversed card. It may be fear of the unknown, of being someone different, of losing the identity that came with a particular chapter. Sometimes people stay in situations that are manifestly not working because the alternative is blank and unmapped. The blankness can be frightening, even when what is behind us is worse.\n\nIf Death appears reversed in your reading, consider what you would have to admit in order to move on, and what you imagine would happen if you did. Often the answer is smaller than the dread. Change rarely requires everything at once. It asks for one honest acknowledgment, and then one step. The card does not tell you to rush. It simply notes that clinging has a cost, and that the cost is usually higher than letting go."],"love":"In love, Death signals the end of a phase: a relationship concluding, a pattern being outgrown, or a partnership transforming into something different. It can also mean releasing an old hurt or an idealized past so that new connection becomes possible. The change may be sad, yet it often brings relief. If you are single, it can describe closing one chapter before beginning another. Reversed, it suggests clinging to what is finished or fear of stepping into something unfamiliar.","career":"At work, Death often indicates the end of a role, project, company phase or career direction. It may describe a restructure, a resignation, a shift into a new field or the sunset of an old approach. Though unsettling, the card tends to support renewal. It asks you to let go of what no longer serves and to prepare for something different. Reversed, it points to staying in a position out of fear or resisting a change that is already underway.","money":"Financially, Death suggests closing an old chapter: ending a financial habit, clearing a debt, restructuring, or releasing a source of income that has dried up. It can mark a reset that makes better habits possible. It is not a prediction of loss as such. Reversed, it indicates holding on to a failing arrangement. Take time to review your finances honestly, and seek professional guidance for major decisions.","advice":"As advice, Death says to stop propping up what has already ended. Name what is finished, allow yourself to feel the loss, and make deliberate room for what comes next. You do not need to have the new chapter planned. Clear the ground first. Be gentle with yourself while you do it, since even welcome endings carry weight.","feelings":"As feelings, Death suggests someone going through a significant internal change. They may be letting go of old attitudes or ending a stage of their own life, which affects how they relate to you. The feeling may be mixed, with grief and relief together. Reversed, they could be clinging to old patterns or struggling with an ending they have not yet accepted.","yes_no":{"answer":"No","why":"Death generally signals that the current path or situation is ending, so the answer to continuing as is leans toward no. It may, however, be a strong yes to a fresh start."}},{"slug":"temperance","name":"Temperance","number":"XIV","element":"Fire","astrology":"Sagittarius","keywords_upright":["Balance and moderation","Patience","Blending opposites","Healing and harmony","Steady purpose"],"keywords_reversed":["Excess or extremes","Imbalance","Impatience","Discord","Lack of long-term vision"],"summary":"Temperance is the art of blending: mixing opposing elements in the right proportion so that something smoother and more durable results. It favors patience, moderation and a steady course over dramatic swings.","upright":["Temperance asks for the middle path, though not a bland or timid one. The angel pours with total attention, and that precision is the point. Two elements are being brought together in proportion, and a successful blend requires patience, sensitivity and a willingness to adjust. When this card appears, it often indicates a time for moderation, steady effort and quiet integration. The loud, extreme options may look more exciting, but the card prefers a sustainable rhythm.\n\nIt also speaks of healing. A person who has been through disruption, especially after the upheavals of the preceding cards, may find in Temperance a gentle recovery period. Opposites that were fighting begin to cooperate: head and heart, work and rest, ambition and contentment. Nothing is forced. You find your balance by listening to what each part of you actually needs and offering it in measured amounts.","There is a long view built into this card. The path in the background leads upward, and the angel does not seem hurried. Waite associated the card with combination and with the way different forces are united through skill and time. In real life, that may look like a gradual project, a slow reconciliation, a new routine that takes weeks to settle, or a compromise that satisfies both sides because each gave up a little.\n\nTemperance also encourages testing and adjusting as you go. A recipe is tasted before it is served. If something is too sharp or too bland, you tweak it instead of throwing it out. In practice, this card invites you to bring that attitude to a current problem. Look for the blend instead of the winner, and trust that consistent, modest action will carry you farther than a burst of intensity followed by collapse."],"reversed":["Reversed, Temperance suggests the blend has gone wrong. Something is out of proportion: too much work, too much indulgence, too much giving, too much control. You may feel tugged between opposing demands without finding a way to reconcile them, or you may be living in a series of extremes, swinging from overcommitment to burnout. The card gently points out that the pattern is not sustainable.\n\nImpatience often sits at the root. Wanting results now can lead to shortcuts, rushed decisions and the abandonment of a sound plan right before it begins to work. If this card shows up reversed, consider where you are skipping steps or asking one area of life to carry the weight that belongs across several.","The reversed card may also describe discord, in which people or priorities pull in separate directions with no mediator. A project can lack coordination, a relationship can lose its give-and-take, or your own values and actions may have drifted apart. The remedy is not dramatic. It is re-proportioning: reducing what is excessive, adding what is missing, and checking regularly that the mixture is working.\n\nThere may also be a lack of long-term vision. Choices made purely for short-term relief tend to cost more over time. Pausing to ask where you are headed, and whether your daily habits move you that way, is often enough to start restoring equilibrium."],"love":"In relationships, Temperance describes harmony built on patience, compromise and genuine listening. It suggests a connection in which each person adjusts without losing themselves, or a healing phase after conflict. It favors slow, steady development over intensity. If you are single, it may point toward a calmer approach to meeting someone, with room for friendship first. Reversed, it points to imbalance, with one person giving too much, or to fiery extremes and difficulty finding common ground.","career":"At work, Temperance favors collaboration, mediation, steady progress and a sustainable pace. It can indicate skillful combining of resources or talents, a role that suits your strengths, or a project that needs patience instead of pressure. Work-life balance may be a theme. Reversed, it suggests overextension, conflicting priorities, rushed work or a team out of sync, and a need to re-establish a workable rhythm.","money":"Financially, Temperance supports budgeting, moderation and a measured plan for the long term. It favors steady saving and careful spending over dramatic gambles. Resources are best managed by blending caution with reasonable growth. Reversed, it points to overspending, imbalance between income and outflow, or impatience with slow results. Seek qualified advice for significant decisions.","advice":"As advice, Temperance says to slow down and find the right proportion. Do not choose between extremes. Look for the blend. Keep a steady routine, adjust in small increments and give things time to mix. If you are exhausted, ease off. If you are stagnating, add a little. Aim for a rhythm you could keep up for months, not days.","feelings":"As feelings, Temperance suggests someone who is calm, considerate and approaching you with care. Their regard may be steady rather than dramatic, and they are likely trying to find a way for you both to fit together. Reversed, they may be conflicted, overwhelmed or struggling to find the right proportion in how they respond.","yes_no":{"answer":"Yes","why":"Temperance leans toward a favorable, gradual outcome, provided you stay patient and moderate. Expect it to unfold over time instead of all at once."}},{"slug":"the-devil","name":"The Devil","number":"XV","element":"Earth","astrology":"Capricorn","keywords_upright":["Attachment and bondage","Temptation","Unhealthy patterns","Shadow side","Material fixation"],"keywords_reversed":["Breaking free","Reclaiming power","Releasing addiction","Awareness of the trap","Detachment"],"summary":"The Devil shows how we bind ourselves to habits, people or beliefs that diminish us, often while believing we have no choice. The chains are loose, and recognizing that is where freedom begins.","upright":["The Devil is the card of entanglement. It describes what we are bound to, and in particular the things we cling to that cost us more than they give. These might be a habit, a relationship, a compulsion, a belief about ourselves, or a relationship with money, work or status that has taken over. The card does not condemn pleasure or desire. It points out the point at which desire becomes a leash.\n\nWhat makes this card so instructive is the looseness of the chains. The two figures appear trapped, but nothing physically prevents them from stepping away. Often the grip is psychological: fear of loneliness, fear of loss, a conviction that this is simply who we are. The Devil asks you to look squarely at the pattern, including the part of you that gets something from it. Shame tends to keep the cycle going, whereas honest recognition begins to loosen it.","There is a shadow dimension here too. The card can indicate drives, fears and desires you would rather not admit to yourself, and which operate more powerfully for being hidden. Facing them without self-punishment is part of the work. In the Golden Dawn system the Devil is tied to Capricorn and the earth element, which fits its themes of material fixation, ambition without limit and the pull of the physical.\n\nThe practical message is to notice where you feel compelled instead of choosing. Ask where you say you must when you could say you would like to. Sometimes the answer is small, such as a habit that could be adjusted. Sometimes it is large and may call for support from friends, counselors or professionals. Either way, the card says that awareness is the first move, and the chain is not as heavy as it looks."],"reversed":["Reversed, the Devil points toward release. You may be seeing a pattern for what it is, stepping away from an unhealthy attachment, or beginning to reclaim power you had given away. The shift is often gradual and sometimes uncomfortable, since leaving a familiar trap can bring withdrawal, doubt and a wish to go back. The card acknowledges that this is part of the process and not a sign that you are failing.\n\nIt can also describe a moment of sharp awareness, when you finally see how a situation has been controlling you. Insight does not always lead to immediate change, but it does shift the balance. Once you can name the hook, you can start deciding what to do about it.","There is a caution to hold alongside the hope. Reversed, the card can point to resistance: you know about the pattern but have not acted, or you are swapping one dependency for another. Detachment can also be mistaken for freedom when it is really avoidance. Look at whether your new choices genuinely serve you or just replace one form of compulsion with another.\n\nSupport matters here. Breaking a long pattern is easier with allies, whether trusted people or qualified professionals, especially where health, addiction or safety are involved. The card does not require you to manage this alone. It simply says the way out exists and that your willingness to look is a real beginning."],"love":"In love, the Devil often points to intense attraction that carries an unhealthy edge: possessiveness, dependency, jealousy or a bond that feels compulsive. It can also describe sexual chemistry and passion that are genuine but need honest boundaries. The card asks whether you are choosing the relationship or simply unable to leave. Reversed, it suggests recognizing a controlling pattern and finding the freedom to step away or renegotiate the terms.","career":"At work, the Devil can reflect feeling trapped in a job, driven by status or money, or caught in a toxic culture. It may point to workaholism, golden handcuffs or an ambition that has become obsessive. Ask what you are getting from the arrangement and what it costs. Reversed, it suggests breaking away, setting limits or reclaiming your autonomy.","money":"Financially, the Devil is associated with materialism, debt, overspending and a sense of being owned by money. It asks whether your finances serve your life or the other way round. Reversed, it points to loosening that grip, paying down obligations or developing a healthier relationship with money. Seek qualified financial guidance for serious difficulties.","advice":"As advice, the Devil says to look squarely at what binds you, without shame. Ask what you get from the pattern and what it costs. Notice the chains that could actually be lifted. Reach for support if you need it. You do not have to dismantle everything today, but you can stop pretending the pattern is not there.","feelings":"As feelings, the Devil suggests strong attraction, obsession or a sense of being pulled in, perhaps against better judgment. It can also describe someone who feels trapped or conflicted about how they feel. Reversed, they may be pulling away from an unhealthy dynamic or becoming more aware of what has been holding them.","yes_no":{"answer":"No","why":"The Devil warns of attachment or compulsion that may not serve you, so it leans toward no, or at least a pause to examine your motives before saying yes."}},{"slug":"the-tower","name":"The Tower","number":"XVI","element":"Fire","astrology":"Mars","keywords_upright":["Sudden upheaval","Collapse of false structures","Revelation","Shock","Forced change"],"keywords_reversed":["Averted disaster","Fear of change","Delayed collapse","Aftershocks","Slow rebuilding"],"summary":"The Tower brings a sudden break in something built on weak foundations, clearing the way for truth and rebuilding. It is jarring, but what falls was rarely sound.","upright":["The Tower is the card of sudden, disruptive change, the sort that arrives without a convenient warning and does not leave things as they were. It often marks the collapse of a structure that had seemed solid: a plan, a belief, a role, a relationship, a story you had been telling. The lightning is a flash of truth, and when it strikes, whatever was built on shaky ground comes down. The experience can be shocking, and the card does not pretend otherwise.\n\nYet there is a quiet mercy in the image. The tower was built to keep something in or out, and it was sealed against reality. The strike cracks it open. In hindsight, many people recognize that the thing that fell had been unsustainable for some time and that part of them already knew. The Tower does not punish. It exposes, and what it exposes was there all along.","The practical task is to survive the shock and to avoid rebuilding the same fragile structure out of habit. After the disruption comes a stretch of clearing and reassessment. Take stock of what remains standing, what you actually value, and what you learned about the weak points. Mars, the planet attached to the card, brings force and urgency, and that force can be steered into decisive action once the dust settles.\n\nBe gentle with yourself in the immediate aftermath. Strong reactions to sudden change are normal, and this is not the moment for sweeping, permanent decisions. Look after basic needs, lean on supportive people and let the situation reveal itself over time. The Tower tends to be followed in the deck by the Star, a reminder that recovery and hope follow disruption, though not on the same day."],"reversed":["Reversed, the Tower may describe a collapse that has been avoided, postponed or experienced as a series of smaller tremors instead of one big strike. You might be sensing instability and doing whatever you can to prop it up. Sometimes that succeeds in buying time. Often it only delays the reckoning and makes it harder, because the underlying cracks remain unaddressed.\n\nThe card can also point to resisting necessary change. If you can feel that a situation needs to be dismantled but you are clinging to it out of fear, the reversal asks whether controlled, voluntary change would be less painful than waiting for a forced one.","Another meaning concerns the aftermath. The Tower reversed may show someone who is processing a past upheaval, still feeling the aftershocks and wondering how to rebuild. Recovery is rarely linear. There can be a lingering fear that disaster will repeat, and a temptation to over-control everything to prevent it.\n\nThe suggestion is to move at a steady pace. Acknowledge what happened, take the lessons that are useful, and rebuild on firmer ground, with honest foundations. You may also find that the thing you feared was less final than it felt in the moment, and that you have more resources than you realized."],"love":"In love, the Tower can signal a sudden revelation, a breakup, an argument that changes everything or the collapse of an illusion about a partner or the relationship. It exposes what was not working or not true. The aftermath, though painful, can clear the way for greater honesty. Reversed, it suggests avoiding a necessary reckoning, lingering aftershocks, or a rocky period survived without full collapse.","career":"At work, the Tower may point to an unexpected change: redundancy, restructure, a failed project, a revealed problem or a sudden shift in direction. It tends to show where the foundations were weak. In time it can free you to pursue something more fitting. Reversed, it may describe a narrowly avoided shake-up, resistance to needed change or a slow recovery from a professional upset.","money":"Financially, the Tower may indicate an unexpected expense, a shock to income or the failure of a shaky arrangement. It is a reminder to build resilience and to verify the foundations of any plan. Reversed, it can suggest a narrowly averted setback or a slow recovery. For serious difficulties, seek qualified professional advice.","advice":"As advice, the Tower says to let what is false fall, and to avoid propping it up out of fear. After a shock, secure the basics, avoid permanent decisions made in panic and observe what remains. When you do rebuild, use the truth you now have. The structure you make next can be sturdier because you know where the weakness was.","feelings":"As feelings, the Tower suggests intense, possibly overwhelming emotions, or a person whose view has been abruptly upended. They may be shaken, reactive or going through a major realization. Reversed, they may be holding strong feelings in, bracing for conflict or still recovering from a recent upheaval.","yes_no":{"answer":"No","why":"The Tower generally points to disruption and the failure of the present plan, so it leans toward no. Often, though, that no clears the way for a better alternative."}},{"slug":"the-star","name":"The Star","number":"XVII","element":"Air","astrology":"Aquarius","keywords_upright":["Hope and renewal","Healing","Inspiration","Serenity","Trust in the future"],"keywords_reversed":["Discouragement","Lost faith","Disconnection","Self-doubt","Hope deferred"],"summary":"The Star is a quiet return of hope after hardship. It offers rest, healing and the sense that you are being guided, and it invites you to trust gently in what is coming.","upright":["After the wreckage of the Tower, the Star arrives like a clear night. Nothing dramatic happens. You simply notice that you can breathe, that the sky is wide, and that some quiet part of you believes things can improve. This is the card of hope in its truest form: not blind optimism, but a steady confidence that rests on having come through something hard. The woman kneels without armor, and that openness is both the cost and the gift of this stage.\n\nIt also carries themes of healing and renewal. The water she pours returns to the pool and nourishes the ground, an image of giving that does not deplete. Rest, creativity, gentle self-care and time in nature all belong to the Star. If you have been running on adrenaline or enduring a long difficult period, the card suggests that replenishment is now allowed, and that you can stop bracing for the next blow.","The Star is also a card of inspiration and guidance. Traditionally tied to Aquarius, it points beyond personal concerns to a larger sense of purpose and connection. You may feel drawn toward a creative project, a cause or a longer-term dream that seems newly possible. The seven small stars and one large star have been read as symbols of influence from beyond the self, a reminder that you are part of something bigger than your immediate troubles.\n\nTreat the hope this card brings as something to cultivate. Small, consistent steps toward what you want, taken calmly, are better than a burst of effort driven by relief. You do not need to know exactly how things will unfold. Allow the light to orient you and let the path appear a few feet at a time. Trust, in this case, is a practice more than a feeling."],"reversed":["Reversed, the Star can show a dimming of hope. You may feel drained, discouraged or cut off from anything that used to inspire you. After a difficult period, it is natural to feel that you have little left to give, and the card acknowledges that. Cynicism can step in as protection, since hoping again feels risky, but it also closes doors that might otherwise open.\n\nThe reversal may also indicate disconnection from your own intuition or sense of direction. Perhaps you cannot see where you are heading or why it matters. This is not a permanent condition. Often it simply reflects exhaustion and a need for rest, quiet and kindness toward yourself before any planning can be done.","Self-doubt is another strand. The reversed Star can describe a loss of faith in your abilities, your path or the possibility of recovery. Comparison with others, or impatience at your own pace, tends to make it worse. The suggestion here is gentle: lower the bar, take the smallest step available, and reconnect with something that feels nourishing, whether a walk, a conversation or a creative act.\n\nHope deferred is still hope. If the light seems faint, remember that stars are always present even when clouds hide them. The card encourages patient self-compassion and does not demand optimism on command. A small flicker is enough to begin with, and it grows when it is tended."],"love":"In love, the Star suggests healing, openness and renewed faith in connection. It can indicate a gentle, sincere relationship, or recovery after heartbreak with a growing readiness to trust again. It supports honesty and a hopeful outlook without pressure. If you are single, it points to being open without rushing. Reversed, it suggests discouragement, fear of being hurt again or difficulty believing that love is possible for you.","career":"At work, the Star favors inspiration, creative projects, a renewed sense of purpose and recovery after a hard stretch. It supports long-term vision, mentoring and work that feels meaningful. Opportunities may appear gradually. Reversed, it suggests burnout, loss of motivation or doubt about your direction, and a need to restore energy before pushing forward.","money":"Financially, the Star suggests gradual improvement and a more hopeful outlook after difficulty. It favors modest, steady steps and planning for the future over dramatic moves. Reversed, it may show discouragement or a loss of confidence in your financial prospects. Seek professional advice for significant decisions.","advice":"As advice, the Star says to rest, restore and let hope return at its own pace. Do things that feel nourishing and honest. Take small steps toward something you care about, without demanding quick proof. Trust that you are allowed to recover, and be kind to yourself while you do.","feelings":"As feelings, the Star suggests warmth, sincerity and quiet hope. The person may see something genuinely promising in you or in the connection, and they approach it with openness. Reversed, they may feel discouraged, withdrawn or unsure whether to trust their own feelings.","yes_no":{"answer":"Yes","why":"The Star is one of the most hopeful cards, pointing to a favorable, healing direction. Expect gradual progress rather than an overnight result."}},{"slug":"the-moon","name":"The Moon","number":"XVIII","element":"Water","astrology":"Pisces","keywords_upright":["Illusion and uncertainty","Intuition","Subconscious fears","Dreams","Things not as they seem"],"keywords_reversed":["Clearing confusion","Releasing fear","Truth emerging","Repressed emotion","Anxiety easing"],"summary":"The Moon shows a path lit only by uncertain light, where intuition, dreams and fear all speak at once. Move carefully, trust what you can verify, and let the picture become clearer before deciding.","upright":["The Moon is the card of the twilight world, where things appear unclear and the imagination fills in what the eye cannot see. It governs dreams, intuition and the subconscious, and it also governs worry, projection and half-seen threats. When it appears, you may be sensing something you cannot yet name, or you may be reading danger into shadows. Both are possible, and the card advises not trusting either impulse too quickly.\n\nThe path in the image continues, which matters. The way forward exists, but you are moving by moonlight, and judgments made at night often change by morning. This is not the moment for dramatic, irreversible choices based on a hunch or a fear. It is a moment to slow down, observe, gather information and notice what your body and your dreams are telling you without treating every sensation as a fact.","There is real value in this card as well. The Moon, attached to Pisces and to water, speaks of imagination, empathy and emotional depth. It can accompany creative work, spiritual exploration and times when the rational mind needs to share the stage with something less tidy. The crayfish emerging from the pool is a reminder that old material surfaces: forgotten feelings, patterns from the past, questions you have not wanted to examine.\n\nA useful approach is to separate what is known from what is feared. Write down the facts, then the stories your mind has added. Talk to someone you trust. Where there is genuine uncertainty, be willing to say you do not know yet. The Moon does not claim that deception is certain, but it warns that appearances may not tell the whole story, and clarity tends to come with time and attention."],"reversed":["Reversed, the Moon often suggests that fog is lifting. Something that was confusing becomes clearer, a worry turns out to be smaller than imagined, or a truth that was hidden comes to light. The relief can be considerable. You may notice that anxieties which felt overwhelming a short while ago are losing their grip.\n\nThe reversal can also point to releasing fear and facing what had been buried. This may mean acknowledging an emotion you pushed down or recognizing a pattern you had been avoiding. It is not always comfortable, but surfacing it is usually healthier than letting it operate unseen.","A different reading is that you are repressing something, whether a feeling, an intuition or a piece of information, and its pressure is building. Confusion may be a symptom of avoidance, and some of the anxiety may be easing only because you are not looking directly. The card gently suggests bringing it into the open, in your own time and with appropriate support.\n\nIt can also describe a lingering unease that has not yet resolved, in which you are less lost than before but not fully clear. Be patient. Keep grounding yourself with routine, rest and honest conversation, and give your picture of the situation a chance to settle before you act on it."],"love":"In love, the Moon points to uncertainty, mixed signals or feelings that are hard to read. It can suggest a connection that is mysterious or emotionally charged, or fears and projections shaping how you see a partner. It advises patience and honest communication over assumptions. Reversed, it suggests confusion clearing, hidden matters coming to light or old fears finally easing.","career":"At work, the Moon indicates unclear circumstances, uncertain direction or situations where not all information is available. It may be wise to observe before committing and to verify what you are told. It can also favor creative and intuitive work. Reversed, it suggests clarity arriving, anxieties about work subsiding or a hidden problem surfacing so it can be handled.","money":"Financially, the Moon advises caution when details are unclear. Verify facts, read terms carefully and avoid decisions made out of fear or on a vague hunch. Reversed, it suggests that confusion is clearing and the picture is becoming more reliable. For significant decisions, consult qualified professionals.","advice":"As advice, the Moon says to go slowly and trust what you can verify. Notice your dreams and instincts without taking every fear as truth. Do not make major decisions in the dark. Ground yourself in routine, talk things through and wait for the light to improve before you commit.","feelings":"As feelings, the Moon suggests emotions that are deep, changeable or difficult to articulate. The person may be confused, uncertain or reluctant to reveal what they feel. There may also be fear shaping their behavior. Reversed, they may be gaining clarity or finally admitting a feeling they had hidden.","yes_no":{"answer":"Maybe","why":"The Moon signals that the picture is unclear or incomplete, so a firm answer is premature. Gather more information and revisit the question when things are clearer."}},{"slug":"the-sun","name":"The Sun","number":"XIX","element":"Fire","astrology":"Sun","keywords_upright":["Joy and vitality","Clarity and success","Warmth","Confidence","Openness"],"keywords_reversed":["Dimmed enthusiasm","Delayed success","Overconfidence","Temporary gloom","Unrealistic optimism"],"summary":"The Sun brings clarity, warmth and plain, uncomplicated happiness. What was hidden is visible, energy returns, and you are encouraged to enjoy what is good and let yourself be seen.","upright":["The Sun is among the most straightforward cards in the deck, and its message is correspondingly simple: things are lit, and they are good. After the uncertainties of the Moon, here is clarity. Doubts clear, energy returns and you can see what is real. In a reading, it often points to success, vitality and a sense of being at ease in your own life. It is the card that says you may enjoy this, without bracing for it to be taken away.\n\nThe child at its center is the key image. He rides without a saddle or reins, relaxed and unselfconscious. The card encourages that kind of openness: authenticity, play, willingness to be seen as you are. Confidence here does not come from performing. It comes from being comfortable, and from allowing warmth, whether from other people or from your own sense of well-being, to land.","The Sun also carries themes of growth and nourishment. The sunflowers rise above a wall that once separated, and the card suggests that barriers are being outgrown. Projects may flourish, relationships may warm and a period of effort may begin to pay off. It tends to encourage generosity, since light shared does not diminish.\n\nStill, even the Sun asks for presence. Enjoying a good moment fully, instead of rushing past it to the next task, is part of its message. If things are going well, let yourself notice it. If things are difficult, the card is a reminder that clarity and relief are possible, and that you are allowed to want and expect them. Let your effort be lighter, your honesty warmer and your gratitude real."],"reversed":["Reversed, the Sun is rarely a card of disaster. It more often describes a temporary dimming: enthusiasm that is lower than usual, a success that is slower in coming or a cheerful outlook that is hard to access right now. The light is still there, but a cloud is in the way. Fatigue, discouragement or minor setbacks may be at the root, and they usually pass.\n\nIt can also point to difficulty enjoying what is good. You may be unable to trust happiness, or you may be so focused on what could go wrong that the present is lost. If so, notice that tendency, and allow yourself small, deliberate pleasures.","Another reading concerns overconfidence or unrealistic optimism. If the Sun is reversed alongside warning signs, it may be urging you to look more carefully at details you are glossing over. Brightness can be blinding, and enthusiasm without any checking can lead to avoidable errors.\n\nThe overall tone remains encouraging. The reversal says the clarity you want may take longer, or require more honest effort, but it is not out of reach. Keep taking sensible steps, ask for feedback and make room for rest. The sun returns, and the sensible gardener keeps tending the soil in the meantime."],"love":"In love, the Sun suggests warmth, openness and genuine happiness. It can indicate a relationship with trust and easy affection, a joyful stage of life together or the clarity that comes from honest communication. For singles, it points to confidence and an inviting presence. It can also relate to children and family. Reversed, it suggests a temporary cloud, guarded feelings or expectations that need to become more realistic.","career":"At work, the Sun favors success, recognition, clarity and energy. It supports visible achievements, a confident presentation of your work and a positive atmosphere. Plans may go well and goals feel attainable. Reversed, it suggests delayed results, flagging motivation or overconfidence that overlooks details. Stay steady and check the fine print.","money":"Financially, the Sun suggests a brighter, clearer outlook, with gains or stability and confidence in your plans. It favors openness and generosity. Reversed, it may indicate slower progress or optimism running ahead of the facts. Keep sensible habits and seek qualified advice for important decisions.","advice":"As advice, the Sun says to let yourself be visible, enjoy what is good and act from openness. Share your news, spend time outdoors, do something playful. Trust the clarity you have found. If something is working, keep it going and give thanks for it, without waiting for the other shoe to drop.","feelings":"As feelings, the Sun suggests warmth, enthusiasm and open affection. The person is likely happy with you or about the situation and is not hiding it. Their feelings tend to be clear and generous. Reversed, they may be feeling low or guarded, or may be showing more optimism than they actually feel.","yes_no":{"answer":"Yes","why":"The Sun is one of the most positive cards, indicating success, clarity and a favorable outcome. Keep your expectations grounded, but this is a strong yes."}},{"slug":"judgement","name":"Judgement","number":"XX","element":"Fire","astrology":"Pluto","keywords_upright":["Awakening and calling","Reckoning","Renewal","Self-forgiveness","Answering a summons"],"keywords_reversed":["Self-doubt","Ignoring the call","Harsh self-judgment","Regret","Avoiding reflection"],"summary":"Judgement is a call to rise: to look honestly at your past, forgive what needs forgiving and answer a deeper summons about who you are becoming. It is less a verdict than an invitation to begin again.","upright":["Judgement is a card of awakening. In the image, a trumpet sounds and people rise to meet it, and in a reading it often suggests that something inside you has been called. It may be a sense of vocation, a realization about how you want to live, or an urge to change course that will no longer be ignored. The call seems to come from beyond the usual noise of daily concerns, and the figures respond by standing up as they are, without preparation or disguise.\n\nThe card also carries the theme of reckoning, though more gently than the name implies. It asks you to review your life, to see patterns, decisions and consequences with clarity and to take responsibility without punishing yourself. The goal is not condemnation but integration. When you can look at your past honestly, you can bring forward what is useful and release what is not.","Forgiveness is central here. Forgiving others, and just as importantly yourself, frees energy that has been tied to old events. Many people meet Judgement at a threshold: after an ending, a hard lesson or a long period of reflection. It marks the moment when you are ready to move into a new phase with a clearer sense of who you are.\n\nThe practical invitation is to answer the call with action. That may mean making a decision you have postponed, having an honest conversation, changing direction in your work, or simply allowing yourself to be seen as you are now. You do not need to know everything in advance. Respond to what is calling, take a first step and let the rest become clear as you go."],"reversed":["Reversed, Judgement can indicate that the call is being ignored or drowned out. You may sense that something needs to change but keep postponing it, distracting yourself or telling yourself that it is not the right time. Fear of what an honest look would reveal is common, and the delay can bring a quiet restlessness or regret.\n\nIt can also describe harsh self-judgment: replaying old mistakes, criticizing yourself with a severity you would never apply to a friend. This kind of judgment keeps the past alive and blocks forward movement. The card suggests that self-compassion is not weakness but a precondition for change.","There may also be a pattern of avoiding reflection, repeating the same cycles without examining them. In this case the lesson has not yet been absorbed, and circumstances may keep presenting it in new forms. Making space for honest review, perhaps through journaling or conversation with someone you trust, can help.\n\nThe reversal does not mean you have missed your chance. The call, in the tradition of this card, can still be answered. What is needed is a willingness to hear it, to forgive what can be forgiven and to take one concrete step in the direction your honest self already points."],"love":"In love, Judgement suggests a moment of reckoning and renewal: reviewing past relationships with clarity, forgiving old wounds or having a significant conversation that changes the direction of a partnership. It can mark a fresh start or a reunion built on honest understanding. Reversed, it suggests dwelling on past hurts, harsh self-criticism or avoiding a conversation that needs to happen.","career":"At work, Judgement points to a calling, a career reassessment or a decision to pursue work that matches who you have become. It may also indicate a performance review or a moment of evaluation. It encourages integrating past experience into a new direction. Reversed, it suggests doubt, ignoring a nudge toward change or being overly harsh about past choices.","money":"Financially, Judgement invites an honest review of your situation, with clear eyes on past patterns and a decision to act on what you find. It can mark a turning point toward a healthier approach. Reversed, it suggests avoiding the numbers or regret about past choices. Seek qualified advice for important decisions.","advice":"As advice, Judgement says to answer the call. Review your past honestly, forgive what you can and act on what you now know. Avoid punishing yourself for old mistakes, since they have already taught what they could. Take one concrete step in the direction that feels true, and let that step be enough for now.","feelings":"As feelings, Judgement suggests someone who is reflecting deeply and may be coming to a clear, honest conclusion about you or the situation. They may be ready to forgive, reconnect or make a significant decision. Reversed, they may be conflicted, self-critical or avoiding facing how they feel.","yes_no":{"answer":"Yes","why":"Judgement leans toward a decisive, renewing yes, especially if you have reflected honestly and feel called to act. Make sure the choice reflects your real values."}},{"slug":"the-world","name":"The World","number":"XXI","element":"Earth","astrology":"Saturn","keywords_upright":["Completion","Wholeness","Fulfillment","Integration","Arrival"],"keywords_reversed":["Unfinished business","Lingering loose ends","Shortcuts","Delayed closure","Stuck near the finish"],"summary":"The World marks completion, the sense that a cycle has been lived through and its pieces now fit together. Celebrate what you have finished, and notice that every ending also opens a larger circle.","upright":["The World is the final card of the Major Arcana and the sense of arrival is unmistakable. A cycle has completed and you see it whole. The effort, the setbacks and the lessons of earlier stages have come together, and there is now a feeling of integration. The dancer is neither rushing nor straining. She is simply, gracefully, in her place. In a reading, it often points to the achievement of a significant goal, the end of a long process or a time when different parts of life feel connected.\n\nCompletion deserves to be marked. Many people move on to the next task without acknowledging what they have finished. The World asks you to pause long enough to recognize what you have done, to thank those who helped and to enjoy the sense of wholeness. It is a card of fulfillment, and it encourages you to take it in without minimizing it.","There is, however, a further layer. The wreath is a circle, and circles continue. Waite described the card as the perfected cycle, and in the tarot sequence the Fool begins again after the World. The ending is therefore also a threshold. What you have integrated becomes the base from which a larger undertaking can start, and the confidence that comes from finishing carries forward.\n\nThe card is traditionally linked with Saturn, planet of structure and time, which reminds us that completion is earned through patience and persistence. If you are near the end of something, it suggests that you finish properly instead of drifting away early. If you are already at the end, allow yourself to rest and appreciate. When you are ready, consider what new circle you would like to draw, now that you carry the experience of this one."],"reversed":["Reversed, the World often indicates something that is nearly complete but not quite. A project lacks its last piece, a chapter is not fully closed or you are hesitant to finish because finishing means facing what comes next. Loose ends can create a lingering sense of incompleteness, and the energy that would be available for new work stays tied up.\n\nIt can also point to shortcuts. If you are tempted to skip a final step or call something done before it is, the card suggests that a little more care now may spare you trouble later.","A second theme is stalled closure. You may have reached a goal and feel oddly flat, or you may be unable to let go of a phase that has run its course. Sometimes it is fear of the blank page that follows. Other times it is a lack of acknowledgment, because no one marked the achievement and you did not either.\n\nThe suggestion is to identify precisely what remains. Often it is smaller than it feels: a conversation, a document, an act of closure. Complete it, mark the moment in some way and give yourself permission to move on. Wholeness is not the absence of loose ends so much as the willingness to tie them off."],"love":"In love, the World suggests fulfillment and a sense of wholeness: a relationship that feels complete and well-integrated, a commitment reaching a milestone or the peace of feeling at home with yourself and a partner. For singles, it can mean self-acceptance, or a cycle of dating ending with clarity. Reversed, it suggests unfinished emotional business, hesitation at a final step or difficulty closing a chapter.","career":"At work, the World signals completion and achievement: a project delivered, a goal reached, recognition earned or a career phase well concluded. It may also indicate international or wide-reaching opportunities. It invites you to acknowledge what you have built before starting anew. Reversed, it suggests last-minute obstacles, loose ends or delay in closing out a role or project.","money":"Financially, the World points to stability, a goal reached or a cycle of effort that has paid off. It supports consolidating gains and planning the next stage with confidence. Reversed, it suggests unfinished arrangements or a goal slightly out of reach. Seek qualified professional advice for important decisions.","advice":"As advice, the World says to finish what is nearly done and to acknowledge what you have achieved. Tie off loose ends. Celebrate in some concrete way, however modest. Then, when you are ready, consider what you want to begin next, carrying the confidence of this completion with you.","feelings":"As feelings, the World suggests contentment and a sense of wholeness about you or the situation. The person may feel that things fit, or that the connection is fulfilling. Reversed, they may feel something is missing, or may be hesitant to close a chapter or commit fully.","yes_no":{"answer":"Yes","why":"The World points to completion and a successful conclusion, so it leans toward yes. It may take a final push, but the cycle appears to be closing well."}}],"positions":[{"title":"How you see yourself now","hint":"The picture of yourself you are carrying into this question."},{"title":"What you truly want","hint":"The wish beneath the practical goals."},{"title":"What you fear","hint":"The worry that quietly shapes your choices."},{"title":"What works in your favour","hint":"A strength or support you can lean on."},{"title":"What stands in your way","hint":"The obstacle, whether outside you or inside."},{"title":"Where this is heading","hint":"The direction of travel if you carry on as you are."}]};
